  "result": "Code Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding - ACL Anthology (https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-emnlp.1231/)\nciteturn7288view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-emnlp.1231/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 282\nL0:   * cite0†About L1:     * cite1†Announcements L2:     * cite2†Communication channels L3:     * cite3†Related work L4:     * cite4†Copyright L5:     * * * *\nL6: \nL7:     * cite5†Credits L8:     * cite6†Volunteer L9:     * cite7†Development L10:     * cite8†Feedback L11:   * cite0†Using L12:     * cite9†Author directory L13:     * cite10†Citing papers L14:     * cite11†Links in the Anthology L15:     * cite12†Data access L16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * cite13†All FAQs L19:     * * * *\nL20: \nL21:     * ###### Details\nL22:     * cite14†Anthology identifiers L23:     * cite15†Names L24:     * cite16†ORCID iDs L25:     * cite17†DOIs L26:     * cite18†Verified authors L27:   * cite0†Contributions L28:     * cite19†Submissions L29:     * cite20†Corrections L30:     * cite21†Maintain author pages L31:     * cite22†Attachments L32: ## cite23†Code Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding L33: \nL34: cite24†Andreas Geert Motzfeldt , cite25†Joakim Edin , cite26†Casper L. Christensen , cite27†Christian Hardmeier , cite28†Lars Maaløe , cite29†Anna Rogers L35: ##### Correct Metadata for\nL36: \nL37: Use this form to create a GitHub issue with structured data describing the correction. You will need a GitHub account. Once you create that issue, the correction will be reviewed by a staff member.\nL38: \nL39: ⚠️ Mobile Users: Submitting this form to create a new issue will only work with github.com, not the GitHub Mobile app.\nL40: Important: The Anthology treat PDFs as authoritative. Please use this form only to correct data that is out of line with the PDF. See cite20†our corrections guidelines if you need to change the PDF.\nL41: \nL42: Title Adjust the title. Retain tags such as <fixed-case>. [Input]\nL43: \nL44: Authors Adjust author names and order to match the PDF.\nL45: \nL46: [Button: Add Author]\nL47: Abstract Correct abstract if needed. Retain XML formatting tags such as <tex-math>. You may use <b>...</b> for bold, <i>...</i> for italic, <u>...</u> for underline, <sc>...</sc> for small-caps, <tt>...<tt> for `typewriter text`, <url>...</url> for URLs, <a href=...> for hyperlinks, and <par/> for paragraph breaks.\nL48: \nL49: Verification against PDF Ensure that the new title/authors match the snapshot below. (If there is no snapshot or it is too small, consult cite0†the PDF .)\nL50: Authors concatenated from the text boxes above:\nL51: \nL52: [Input] ALL author names match the snapshot above—including middle initials, hyphens, and accents.\nL53: \nL54: [Button: Create GitHub issue for staff review]\nL55: \nL56: * * *\nL57: ##### Abstract\nL58: In medical coding, experts map unstructured clinical notes to alphanumeric codes for diagnoses and procedures. We introduce ‘Code Like Humans’: a new agentic framework for medical coding with large language models. It implements official coding guidelines for human experts, and it is the first solution that can support the full ICD-10 coding system (+70K labels). It achieves the best performance to date on rare diagnosis codes.\nL59: Fine-tuned discriminative classifiers retain an advantage for high-frequency codes, to which they are limited. Towards future work, we also contribute an analysis of system performance and identify its ‘blind spots’ (codes that are systematically undercoded).\nL60: Anthology ID:\nL61:     2025.findings-emnlp.1231\nL62: Volume:\nL63:     cite30†Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2025 L64: Month:\nL65:     November\nL66: Year:\nL67:     2025\nL68: Address:\nL69:     Suzhou, China\nL70: Editors:\nL71:     cite31†Christos Christodoulopoulos , cite32†Tanmoy Chakraborty , cite33†Carolyn Rose , cite34†Violet Peng L72: Venue:\nL73:     cite35†Findings L74: SIG:\nL75: \nL76: Publisher:\nL77:     Association for Computational Linguistics\nL78: Note:\nL79: \nL80: Pages:\nL81:     22612–22627\nL82: Language:\nL83: \nL84: URL:\nL85:     cite36†https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-emnlp.1231/ L86: DOI:\nL87:     cite37†10.18653/v1/2025.findings-emnlp.1231†doi.org L88: Bibkey:\nL89: \nL90: Cite (ACL):\nL91:     Andreas Geert Motzfeldt, Joakim Edin, Casper L. Christensen, Christian Hardmeier, Lars Maaløe, and Anna Rogers. 2025. cite36†Code Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding . In Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2025, pages 22612–22627, Suzhou, China. Association for Computational Linguistics.\nL92: Cite (Informal):\nL93:     cite36†Code Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding (Motzfeldt et al., Findings 2025)\nL94: Copy Citation:\nL95:     [Button: More options…]\nL96: PDF:\nL97:     cite23†https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-emnlp.1231.pdf L98: Checklist:\nL99:     cite38† 2025.findings-emnlp.1231.checklist.pdf L100: cite23†PDF cite0†Cite cite38†Checklist cite0†Fix data L101: \nL102: * * *\nL103: ##### Export citation\nL104: \nL105:     @inproceedings{motzfeldt-etal-2025-code,\nL106:         title = \"Code Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding\",\nL107:         author = \"Motzfeldt, Andreas Geert  and\nL108:           Edin, Joakim  and\nL109:           Christensen, Casper L.  and\nL110:           Hardmeier, Christian  and\nL111:           Maal{\\o}e, Lars  and\nL112:           Rogers, Anna\",\nL113:         editor = \"Christodoulopoulos, Christos  and\nL114:           Chakraborty, Tanmoy  and\nL115:           Rose, Carolyn  and\nL116:           Peng, Violet\",\nL117:         booktitle = \"Findings of the {A}ssociation for {C}omputational {L}inguistics: {EMNLP} 2025\",\nL118:         month = nov,\nL119:         year = \"2025\",\nL120:         address = \"Suzhou, China\",\nL121:         publisher = \"Association for Computational Linguistics\",\nL122:         url = \"https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-emnlp.1231/\",\nL123:         doi = \"10.18653/v1/2025.findings-emnlp.1231\",\nL124:         pages = \"22612--22627\",\nL125:         ISBN = \"979-8-89176-335-7\",\nL126:         abstract = \"In medical coding, experts map unstructured clinical notes to alphanumeric codes for diagnoses and procedures. We introduce `Code Like Humans': a new agentic framework for medical coding with large language models. It implements official coding guidelines for human experts, and it is the first solution that can support the full ICD-10 coding system (+70K labels). It achieves the best performance to date on rare diagnosis codes.\nL127: Fine-tuned discriminative classifiers retain an advantage for high-frequency codes, to which they are limited. Towards future work, we also contribute an analysis of system performance and identify its `blind spots' (codes that are systematically undercoded).\"\nL128:     }\nL129: Download as File\nL130: \nL131:     <?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\nL132:     <modsCollection xmlns=\"http://www.loc.gov/mods/v3\">\nL133:     <mods ID=\"motzfeldt-etal-2025-code\">\nL134:         <titleInfo>\nL135:             <title>Code Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding</title>\nL136:         </titleInfo>\nL137:         <name type=\"personal\">\nL138:             <namePart type=\"given\">Andreas</namePart>\nL139:             <namePart type=\"given\">Geert</namePart>\nL140:             <namePart type=\"family\">Motzfeldt</namePart>\nL141:             <role>\nL142:                 <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">author</roleTerm>\nL143:             </role>\nL144:         </name>\nL145:         <name type=\"personal\">\nL146:             <namePart type=\"given\">Joakim</namePart>\nL147:             <namePart type=\"family\">Edin</namePart>\nL148:             <role>\nL149:                 <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">author</roleTerm>\nL150:             </role>\nL151:         </name>\nL152:         <name type=\"personal\">\nL153:             <namePart type=\"given\">Casper</namePart>\nL154:             <namePart type=\"given\">L</namePart>\nL155:             <namePart type=\"family\">Christensen</namePart>\nL156:             <role>\nL157:                 <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">author</roleTerm>\nL158:             </role>\nL159:         </name>\nL160:         <name type=\"personal\">\nL161:             <namePart type=\"given\">Christian</namePart>\nL162:             <namePart type=\"family\">Hardmeier</namePart>\nL163:             <role>\nL164:                 <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">author</roleTerm>\nL165:             </role>\nL166:         </name>\nL167:         <name type=\"personal\">\nL168:             <namePart type=\"given\">Lars</namePart>\nL169:             <namePart type=\"family\">Maaløe</namePart>\nL170:             <role>\nL171:                 <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">author</roleTerm>\nL172:             </role>\nL173:         </name>\nL174:         <name type=\"personal\">\nL175:             <namePart type=\"given\">Anna</namePart>\nL176:             <namePart type=\"family\">Rogers</namePart>\nL177:             <role>\nL178:                 <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">author</roleTerm>\nL179:             </role>\nL180:         </name>\nL181:         <originInfo>\nL182:             <dateIssued>2025-11</dateIssued>\nL183:         </originInfo>\nL184:         <typeOfResource>text</typeOfResource>\nL185:         <relatedItem type=\"host\">\nL186:             <titleInfo>\nL187:                 <title>Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2025</title>\nL188:             </titleInfo>\nL189:             <name type=\"personal\">\nL190:                 <namePart type=\"given\">Christos</namePart>\nL191:                 <namePart type=\"family\">Christodoulopoulos</namePart>\nL192:                 <role>\nL193:                     <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">editor</roleTerm>\nL194:                 </role>\nL195:             </name>\nL196:             <name type=\"personal\">\nL197:                 <namePart type=\"given\">Tanmoy</namePart>\nL198:                 <namePart type=\"family\">Chakraborty</namePart>\nL199:                 <role>\nL200:                     <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">editor</roleTerm>\nL201:                 </role>\nL202:             </name>\nL203:             <name type=\"personal\">\nL204:                 <namePart type=\"given\">Carolyn</namePart>\nL205:                 <namePart type=\"family\">Rose</namePart>\nL206:                 <role>\nL207:                     <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">editor</roleTerm>\nL208:                 </role>\nL209:             </name>\nL210:             <name type=\"personal\">\nL211:                 <namePart type=\"given\">Violet</namePart>\nL212:                 <namePart type=\"family\">Peng</namePart>\nL213:                 <role>\nL214:                     <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">editor</roleTerm>\nL215:                 </role>\nL216:             </name>\nL217:             <originInfo>\nL218:                 <publisher>Association for Computational Linguistics</publisher>\nL219:                 <place>\nL220:                     <placeTerm type=\"text\">Suzhou, China</placeTerm>\nL221:                 </place>\nL222:             </originInfo>\nL223:             <genre authority=\"marcgt\">conference publication</genre>\nL224:             <identifier type=\"isbn\">979-8-89176-335-7</identifier>\nL225:         </relatedItem>\nL226:         <abstract>In medical coding, experts map unstructured clinical notes to alphanumeric codes for diagnoses and procedures. We introduce ‘Code Like Humans’: a new agentic framework for medical coding with large language models. It implements official coding guidelines for human experts, and it is the first solution that can support the full ICD-10 coding system (+70K labels). It achieves the best performance to date on rare diagnosis codes.\nL227: Fine-tuned discriminative classifiers retain an advantage for high-frequency codes, to which they are limited. Towards future work, we also contribute an analysis of system performance and identify its ‘blind spots’ (codes that are systematically undercoded).</abstract>\nL228:         <identifier type=\"citekey\">motzfeldt-etal-2025-code</identifier>\nL229:         <identifier type=\"doi\">10.18653/v1/2025.findings-emnlp.1231</identifier>\nL230:         <location>\nL231:             <url>https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-emnlp.1231/</url>\nL232:         </location>\nL233:         <part>\nL234:             <date>2025-11</date>\nL235:             <extent unit=\"page\">\nL236:                 <start>22612</start>\nL237:                 <end>22627</end>\nL238:             </extent>\nL239:         </part>\nL240:     </mods>\nL241:     </modsCollection>\nL242: Download as File\nL243: \nL244:     %0 Conference Proceedings\nL245:     %T Code Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding\nL246:     %A Motzfeldt, Andreas Geert\nL247:     %A Edin, Joakim\nL248:     %A Christensen, Casper L.\nL249:     %A Hardmeier, Christian\nL250:     %A Maaløe, Lars\nL251:     %A Rogers, Anna\nL252:     %Y Christodoulopoulos, Christos\nL253:     %Y Chakraborty, Tanmoy\nL254:     %Y Rose, Carolyn\nL255:     %Y Peng, Violet\nL256:     %S Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2025\nL257:     %D 2025\nL258:     %8 November\nL259:     %I Association for Computational Linguistics\nL260:     %C Suzhou, China\nL261:     %@ 979-8-89176-335-7\nL262:     %F motzfeldt-etal-2025-code\nL263:     %X In medical coding, experts map unstructured clinical notes to alphanumeric codes for diagnoses and procedures. We introduce ‘Code Like Humans’: a new agentic framework for medical coding with large language models. It implements official coding guidelines for human experts, and it is the first solution that can support the full ICD-10 coding system (+70K labels). It achieves the best performance to date on rare diagnosis codes.\nL264: Fine-tuned discriminative classifiers retain an advantage for high-frequency codes, to which they are limited. Towards future work, we also contribute an analysis of system performance and identify its ‘blind spots’ (codes that are systematically undercoded).\nL265:     %R 10.18653/v1/2025.findings-emnlp.1231\nL266:     %U https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-emnlp.1231/\nL267:     %U https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.findings-emnlp.1231\nL268:     %P 22612-22627\nL269: Download as File\nL270: ##### Markdown (Informal)\nL271: \nL272: [Code Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding](https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-emnlp.1231/) (Motzfeldt et al., Findings 2025)\nL273: \nL274:   * cite36†Code Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding (Motzfeldt et al., Findings 2025)\nL275: ##### ACL\nL276: \nL277:   * Andreas Geert Motzfeldt, Joakim Edin, Casper L. Christensen, Christian Hardmeier, Lars Maaløe, and Anna Rogers. 2025. cite36†Code Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding . In Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2025, pages 22612–22627, Suzhou, China. Association for Computational Linguistics.\nL278: ACL materials are Copyright © 1963–2026 ACL; other materials are copyrighted by their respective copyright holders. Materials prior to 2016 here are licensed under the cite39†Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 3.0 International License†creativecommons.org . Permission is granted